#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a68d9a02-dbef-5dd5-bcbe-d152d98e32eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349634} {:answer/id #uuid "38a328c6-dc17-5bbe-8857-154506c513dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349635} {:answer/id #uuid "27d29702-bb30-5b8c-9723-8aeb13ad95e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349636} {:answer/id #uuid "d8de15ee-1e09-5e0a-b708-ef65b709f64b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349637} {:answer/id #uuid "65ee09be-f50a-5efb-b77a-0e5717e3379d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8de15ee-1e09-5e0a-b708-ef65b709f64b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349637} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e737d57-b475-589a-ad59-4e2b310035ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5a1609b-f9e4-55ff-a950-03f5b52f54d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-104625" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ for $x\\in [1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2)$ and $(2,9).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(2,9)-f(1,2) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot 1-{e}^{1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aba82fcd-fc34-5a51-8171-230b06bada9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349641} {:answer/id #uuid "c8dce52f-ef25-5b79-9f9d-595d227c2f00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349642} {:answer/id #uuid "a1dbaa58-cea8-5cd7-9f52-fdfd959d5c06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349643} {:answer/id #uuid "02599dd7-3059-5025-b594-e02eddc40139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349644} {:answer/id #uuid "c75e5132-e9c8-5830-b0ea-b7307fef3fa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8dce52f-ef25-5b79-9f9d-595d227c2f00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349642} :answer-field/id #uuid "20c61aeb-762b-581e-8b5d-0aec98ee5142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc48dcc1-4f64-502c-acc3-48a296354b8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104731" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(2xy-y)\\,i+({x}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2xy-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 3)-({0}^{2}\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =48-12 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19037058-b9b6-54eb-bd2f-e3337a19104b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349648} {:answer/id #uuid "740e2bf3-edc9-5461-8b34-c7666d1850aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349649} {:answer/id #uuid "dc80d883-65bb-5ae8-8591-c2e7cc55abf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349650} {:answer/id #uuid "d15fca75-8925-5cb9-8917-46e36a98191e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349651} {:answer/id #uuid "66142dfe-8517-5a0d-b903-31c4ebe88219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66142dfe-8517-5a0d-b903-31c4ebe88219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349652} :answer-field/id #uuid "d78b716a-32a8-5831-a13e-9e2b296d092c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d98baf4b-12a4-56ec-8adc-f870e121a413" :question/math-academy-id "q-124034" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $2x=\\pi {y}^{3}$ for $y\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{\\pi {t}^{3}}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡1-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8a16a3d-cfe7-5bfa-a1fb-f9f84b6c3dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π^{2}(π^{3} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131409} {:answer/id #uuid "24a736ca-ab16-5778-977b-8fa9c0f6c9a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π^{2}(π^{2} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131410} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0860d1-45b2-5d89-8797-b38480f33f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π^{5} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131411} {:answer/id #uuid "42d978e4-529d-53d1-b223-433042417492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π^{2}(π^{3} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131412} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee6e89e-6f9f-5a4b-ab6e-3923079fbe9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π(π^{3} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42d978e4-529d-53d1-b223-433042417492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π^{2}(π^{3} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131412} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e98339e-2e40-5eda-a492-953a304156ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "119e34da-cc6f-596d-82b2-8381c511738d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39749" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{3}-2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{3}-2⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}\\cdot ({\\pi }^{3}-3)-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{3}-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96cc8054-b115-5851-9a73-ce2298cde6b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133359} {:answer/id #uuid "ad58c684-13d9-5a57-b1ac-6317c0998bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133360} {:answer/id #uuid "1f24037b-7501-5452-a3ca-5bb8dc32b5f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133361} {:answer/id #uuid "229aca60-772e-5732-9794-c6fd86f33d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133362} {:answer/id #uuid "62d7827e-46f9-5add-9d93-341cb14ee91e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96cc8054-b115-5851-9a73-ce2298cde6b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133359} :answer-field/id #uuid "1756ffd6-2f65-5aaf-9d45-82e35b571171" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff7ad628-63c0-5640-b2d2-82a36608b04e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98995" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =x{y}^{2}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
